        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 12,
        "titulo": "The present of -er verbs",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: verbs in this group end in «-er» in the infinitive: «comer», «beber», «aprender». Drop the «-er» and you get the stem: «com-», «beb-», «aprend-». STEP 2: add the present-tense endings to the stem: -o, -es, -e, -emos, -éis, -en. «Como, comes, come, comemos, coméis, comen.» STEP 3: compare with the -ar verbs from lesson 4: the vowel changes («habl-a» becomes «com-e»), but the logic is the same. STEP 4: the stress falls on the stem (CO-mo, CO-mes, CO-me, CO-men), except in «nosotros» and «vosotros», where it falls on the ending: «co-ME-mos», «co-MÉ-is». STEP 5: «usted» takes the «él» form («Usted bebe»), and «ustedes» the «ellos» form («Ustedes comen»). «Vosotros» (Spain) carries an accent: «coméis». STEP 6: «leer» has two vowels in a row, and that's regular for this verb: «leo, lees, lee, leemos, leéis, leen». Pronounce every «e» in «lees» and «leemos» separately. STEP 7: in Spain, «comer» can mean to have lunch, and «la comida» can also mean lunch; «cenar» is to have dinner. STEP 8: «aprender a» + infinitive means «to learn to do something»: «Aprendo a cocinar.» STEP 9: «comprender» means to understand; don't confuse it with «comprar» (to buy).",
        "comparacao": "English present tense doesn't change by person the way Spanish does, so the full six-ending set («-o, -es, -e, -emos, -éis, -en») is new material to learn rather than something to map from English. The pronunciation habit worth building: always pronounce the final «e» of «come» as a full, open vowel, not clipped the way an unstressed English ending might be.",
